From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Project Luma <maintainers@projectluma.org>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 23:30:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] scan: Prefer a usable higher band over a stronger lower band

wpa_scan_result_compar() compares the throughput estimate only when the
SNRs of two BSSs, adjusted for channel width, are within 7 dB, or when
either BSS is on 6 GHz. Otherwise the stronger BSS wins. On a dual-band AP
the 2.4 GHz BSS is commonly 10-20 dB stronger than the 5 GHz BSS of the
same AP, and an 80 MHz channel lowers the adjusted 5 GHz SNR by a further
6 dB, so a station picks 2.4 GHz even when 5 GHz would give a good link
with several times the throughput. The 6 GHz exception has the opposite
problem: a weak 6 GHz BSS with a wide channel wins on its estimate alone,
however close it is to unusable.

Compare BSSs in two tiers. A BSS with at least USABLE_SNR (20 dB over
20 MHz: about -72 dBm on 5 and 6 GHz and -69 dBm on 2.4 GHz with the
default noise floors) is preferred over one below it. Between two usable
BSSs the throughput estimate decides, which already accounts for band,
channel width, spatial streams and SNR, then the higher band, then SNR.
Below the usable SNR the previous rules apply, without the 6 GHz
exception.

Example (2.4 GHz HE20 at -45 dBm, 5 GHz VHT80 at -68 dBm, same AP): the
station used to select 2.4 GHz; it now selects 5 GHz.
---
 wpa_supplicant/scan.c | 37 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++--------
 wpa_supplicant/scan.h |  9 +++++++++
 2 files changed, 38 insertions(+), 8 deletions(-)

diff --git a/wpa_supplicant/scan.c b/wpa_supplicant/scan.c
index 8b59e40..77de559 100644
--- a/wpa_supplicant/scan.c
+++ b/wpa_supplicant/scan.c
@@ -2451,14 +2451,35 @@ static int wpa_scan_result_compar(const void *a, const void *b)
 			return 1;
 	}
 
-	/* If SNR is close, decide by max rate or frequency band. For cases
-	 * involving the 6 GHz band, use the throughput estimate irrespective
-	 * of the SNR difference since the LPI/VLP rules may result in
-	 * significant differences in SNR for cases where the estimated
-	 * throughput can be considerably higher with the lower SNR. */
-	if (snr_a && snr_b && (abs(snr_b - snr_a) < 7 ||
-			       is_6ghz_freq(wa->freq) ||
-			       is_6ghz_freq(wb->freq))) {
+	/*
+	 * When both BSSs would give a usable link, the throughput estimate
+	 * decides: it already accounts for band, channel width, spatial
+	 * streams and the SNR, so a usable 5 GHz or 6 GHz BSS is preferred
+	 * over a stronger 2.4 GHz BSS that would carry less traffic. A usable
+	 * BSS is preferred over one that is not, so a weak 6 GHz BSS with a
+	 * wide channel does not win on its estimate alone. Below the usable
+	 * SNR, signal strength matters most.
+	 */
+	if (wa->flags & wb->flags & WPA_SCAN_LEVEL_DBM) {
+		bool usable_a = wa->snr >= USABLE_SNR;
+		bool usable_b = wb->snr >= USABLE_SNR;
+
+		if (usable_a && usable_b) {
+			if (wa->est_throughput != wb->est_throughput)
+				return (int) wb->est_throughput -
+					(int) wa->est_throughput;
+			if (is_6ghz_freq(wa->freq) ^ is_6ghz_freq(wb->freq))
+				return is_6ghz_freq(wa->freq) ? -1 : 1;
+			if (IS_5GHZ(wa->freq) ^ IS_5GHZ(wb->freq))
+				return IS_5GHZ(wa->freq) ? -1 : 1;
+			return snr_b_full - snr_a_full;
+		}
+		if (usable_a != usable_b)
+			return usable_a ? -1 : 1;
+	}
+
+	/* If SNR is close, decide by max rate or frequency band. */
+	if (snr_a && snr_b && abs(snr_b - snr_a) < 7) {
 		if (wa->est_throughput != wb->est_throughput)
 			return (int) wb->est_throughput -
 				(int) wa->est_throughput;
diff --git a/wpa_supplicant/scan.h b/wpa_supplicant/scan.h
index d4c06c1..b4d04dd 100644
--- a/wpa_supplicant/scan.h
+++ b/wpa_supplicant/scan.h
@@ -30,6 +30,15 @@
  */
 #define GREAT_SNR 25
 
+/*
+ * A link with at least this SNR (measured over 20 MHz) is usable for normal
+ * traffic: about -72 dBm on 5 GHz and 6 GHz and -69 dBm on 2.4 GHz with the
+ * default noise floors, which is MCS 3-4 at 80 MHz. Between usable BSSs the
+ * throughput estimate decides, so a usable 5 GHz or 6 GHz BSS is preferred
+ * over a stronger 2.4 GHz BSS that would carry less traffic.
+ */
+#define USABLE_SNR 20
+
 /*
  * IEEE Sts 802.11ax-2021, 9.4.2.161 (Transmit Power Envelope element) indicates
  * no max TX power limit if Maximum Transmit Power field is 63.5 dBm.
